# Migrate from svelte-virtual-scroll-list

[svelte-virtual-scroll-list](https://github.com/v1ack/svelte-virtual-scroll-list) is no longer maintained. Its last release, 1.3.0, came out in July 2023 and uses Svelte 3 slot syntax. This guide moves a list to **@humanspeak/svelte-virtual-list**, a maintained Svelte 5 component that also measures rows of different sizes.

For a side-by-side feature summary, see the [svelte-virtual-scroll-list comparison](/compare/svelte-virtual-scroll-list).

## Before you start

Two features have no equivalent yet:

- **`pageMode`**, which scrolls the list with the whole page. Svelte Virtual List always scrolls inside its own container. If you rely on `pageMode`, stay on svelte-virtual-scroll-list for now.
- **The `top` event** for loading older items at the top. For chat-style lists, use [@humanspeak/svelte-virtual-chat](https://virtualchat.svelte.page), which loads older messages through `onNeedHistory` and keeps your place while they are added.

Everything else maps directly.

## Install

```bash
npm uninstall svelte-virtual-scroll-list
npm install @humanspeak/svelte-virtual-list
```

Svelte Virtual List requires Svelte 5.

## Before and after

A list with a header, a footer, and more rows loaded at the bottom.

**Before**, with svelte-virtual-scroll-list:

```svelte
<script>
    import VirtualScroll from 'svelte-virtual-scroll-list'

    let items = [{ id: 1, text: 'one' } /* ... */]

    async function loadMore() {
        items = [...items, ...(await fetchNextPage())]
    }
</script>

<div style="height: 500px">
    <VirtualScroll
        data={items}
        key="id"
        estimateSize={50}
        bottomThreshold={200}
        on:bottom={loadMore}
        let:data
        let:index
    >
        <div slot="header">Messages</div>
        <div class="row">{index}: {data.text}</div>
        <div slot="footer">Loading…</div>
    </VirtualScroll>
</div>
```

**After**, with Svelte Virtual List:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import VirtualList from '@humanspeak/svelte-virtual-list'

    let items = $state([{ id: 1, text: 'one' } /* ... */])
    let hasMore = $state(true)

    async function loadMore() {
        const page = await fetchNextPage()
        items = [...items, ...page]
        if (page.length === 0) hasMore = false
    }
</script>

<div style="height: 500px">
    <VirtualList
        {items}
        itemKey={(item) => item.id}
        defaultEstimatedItemSize={50}
        loadMoreThreshold={10}
        onLoadMore={loadMore}
        {hasMore}
    >
        {#snippet header()}
            <div>Messages</div>
        {/snippet}
        {#snippet renderItem(item, index)}
            <div class="row">{index}: {item.text}</div>
        {/snippet}
        {#snippet footer()}
            <div>Loading…</div>
        {/snippet}
    </VirtualList>
</div>
```

The list fills its parent, so keep a wrapper with a height, as before.

## Props

| svelte-virtual-scroll-list | Svelte Virtual List | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `data` | `items` | |
| `key="id"` | `itemKey={(item) => item.id}` | A function, so keys can be computed. Set it whenever items are added, removed, or reordered. |
| `estimateSize` | `defaultEstimatedItemSize` | Default `40` instead of `50`. Rows are still measured after they render. |
| `isHorizontal` | `orientation="horizontal"` | Can change at runtime. Horizontal mode is left-to-right only. |
| `keeps` | `bufferSize` | Not the same number: `keeps` is the total rows rendered, `bufferSize` is the extra rows rendered beyond the visible ones. The default usually works. |
| `bottomThreshold` | `loadMoreThreshold` | Counted in items from the end, not pixels. |
| `start` | `scroll({ index })` | See Scroll position, below. |
| `offset` | `scrollToOffset({ offset })` | See Scroll position, below. |
| `pageMode` | None | See Before you start, above. |
| `topThreshold` | None | See Before you start, above. |

## Slots

| svelte-virtual-scroll-list | Svelte Virtual List |
|---|---|
| Default slot with `let:data` and `let:index` | `renderItem` snippet, `(item, index)` |
| `slot="header"` | `header` snippet |
| `slot="footer"` | `footer` snippet |

The header and footer are measured like rows, so resizing them keeps your reading position.

## Events

| svelte-virtual-scroll-list | Svelte Virtual List |
|---|---|
| `on:bottom` | `onLoadMore`, plus `hasMore` set to `false` when there is no more data |
| `on:top` | None. See Before you start, above. |
| `on:scroll` | `onRangeChange`, called with `{ start, end, atTop, atBottom }` when the rendered range or an edge changes rather than on every scroll event |

Unlike `on:bottom`, `onLoadMore` is not called again while a load is still running, or straight after a load that added nothing. See [onLoadMore](/docs/api/events).

## Methods

Get the component with `bind:this`, as before.

| svelte-virtual-scroll-list | Svelte Virtual List |
|---|---|
| `scrollToIndex(index)` | `scroll({ index })` |
| `scrollToOffset(offset)` | `scrollToOffset({ offset })` |
| `scrollToBottom()` | `scroll({ index: items.length - 1, align: 'bottom' })` |
| `getSize`, `getSizes`, `getOffset`, `getClientSize`, `getScrollSize` | None as methods. `debugFunction` reports `totalHeight` and `averageItemHeight`. |
| `updatePageModeFront()` | None, since there is no page mode. |

`scroll()` also takes `align` (`'auto'`, `'top'`, `'bottom'`, `'center'` or `'nearest'`) and `smoothScroll`, and both methods return a promise that resolves when scrolling finishes. See [Methods](/docs/api/methods).

## Scroll position

In svelte-virtual-scroll-list, `start` and `offset` are props, and changing them scrolls the list. Call the methods from an effect for the same behavior:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import VirtualList from '@humanspeak/svelte-virtual-list'

    type Item = { id: number; text: string }

    let { items, start = 0 }: { items: Item[]; start?: number } = $props()
    let list: VirtualList<Item>

    $effect(() => {
        list.scroll({ index: start, align: 'top', smoothScroll: false })
    })
</script>

<VirtualList bind:this={list} {items}>
    {#snippet renderItem(item)}
        <div>{item.text}</div>
    {/snippet}
</VirtualList>
```

